> A total OT thought:  The above is why English is such a crazy language.
> Putting emphasis on different words totally changes the implied meaning. 

Yes, changing the emphasis changes the meaning, but it's not specific to
English, the same could probably be seen in any language.

> Compare:
> *You* lost the pickaxe?!
> You *lost* the pickaxe?!
> You lost *the* pickaxe?!
> You lost the *pickaxe*?!

Emphasis in "the", like in the text you quoted, is how I would think about
it, too. 

*You*: anybody might accidentally drop a pick-axe and not notice it
immediately. Could happen to anybody. I would use this emphasis only for
somebody who is constantly praising how great it is to have a pick-axe.

*lost*: Well.. when you survive far enough in the game, it is not
surprising that you might accidentally misplace something.

*the*: Yeah, there *are* usually many pick-axes in the game to be picked
 up. (May require killing a dwarf, which may be a minor alignment penalty
 depending on your race and alignment, but no big deal.) So what 
 makes the lost one special? This is what I would wonder.

*pickaxe*: Hmm.. very much the same things apply here than what I wrote
 about having the emphasis on "you". One can misplace any object, a
 pick-axe is not easier or harder to accidentally drop than anything else.
 (Well, it actually depends on the way one normally carries the pick-axe..
 it's harder to steal from a bag than from your inventory.<9

